{
  "case_id": "2006-06-484",
  "document_hash": "d939ed6653e40bf096f9ba9f67ecd3f5e345bc6f3c37a2d6de104fb5b3324c19",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Carter G. Phillips",
      "case_id": "2006-06-484",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d939ed6653e40bf096f9ba9f67ecd3f5e345bc6f3c37a2d6de104fb5b3324c19",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d939ed6653e4-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. PHILLIPS: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court:\nIn 1995 Congress acted decisively to curb\nabusive private securities litigation. It took the\nextraordinary step of rejecting categorically the\ntraditional rule of notice pleading in complaints that\nare filed under the securities laws. Instead it\ndeclared that, and this is at page 2 of our petition,\n\"The complaint shall state with particularity facts\ngiving rise to a strong inference that the defendant\nacted with the required state of mind.\""
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2006-06-484",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d939ed6653e40bf096f9ba9f67ecd3f5e345bc6f3c37a2d6de104fb5b3324c19",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d939ed6653e4-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: We'll hear argument\nthis morning in case 06-484, Tellabs, Inc. versus Makor\nIssues & Rights."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Kannon K. Shanmugam",
      "case_id": "2006-06-484",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d939ed6653e40bf096f9ba9f67ecd3f5e345bc6f3c37a2d6de104fb5b3324c19",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d939ed6653e4-0000-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 17,
        "start": 17
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. SHANMUGAM: Thank you Mr. Chief Justice,\nand may it please the Court:\nWhile meritorious private actions are an\nessential supplement to Government enforcement of the\nsecurities laws, abusive action impose substantial costs\non companies and their shareholders. As a cornerstone\nof its effort in the Reform Act to address the problem\nof abusive actions, Congress adopted uniform and more\nstringent pleading requirements including the strong\ninference requirement at issue in this case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2006-06-484",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d939ed6653e40bf096f9ba9f67ecd3f5e345bc6f3c37a2d6de104fb5b3324c19",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d939ed6653e4-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: -- there was a pleading stage, I would like your clear view on how much that changes."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2006-06-484",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d939ed6653e40bf096f9ba9f67ecd3f5e345bc6f3c37a2d6de104fb5b3324c19",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d939ed6653e4-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "do believe that Congress abrogated the background rule that the allegations must be read in the light most favorable to the plaintiff, or as some courts have put it, that all reasonable inferences that can be drawn from the complaint should be drawn in the plaintiff's favor."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2006-06-484",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d939ed6653e40bf096f9ba9f67ecd3f5e345bc6f3c37a2d6de104fb5b3324c19",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d939ed6653e4-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOUTER: Why don't we simply assume that the read most favorable to the plaintiff rule is still in place, but that reading it most favorably to the plaintiff, it must rise to the level of supporting the strong inference?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2006-06-484",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d939ed6653e40bf096f9ba9f67ecd3f5e345bc6f3c37a2d6de104fb5b3324c19",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d939ed6653e4-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Even if there is no Seventh Amendment problem, what sense would it make to have a regime that says plaintiff has to plead more than the plaintiff has to show at summary judgment or prove at trial."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2006-06-484",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d939ed6653e40bf096f9ba9f67ecd3f5e345bc6f3c37a2d6de104fb5b3324c19",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d939ed6653e4-0001-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The requisite scienter is either intent or recklessness, with regard to the underlying conduct at issue, in effect --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2006-06-484",
      "confidence": 0.93,
      "document_hash": "d939ed6653e40bf096f9ba9f67ecd3f5e345bc6f3c37a2d6de104fb5b3324c19",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d939ed6653e4-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOUTER: That is -- that is an argument for the weight of considering motive rather than the relevance of the motive consideration per se."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2006-06-484",
      "confidence": 0.92,
      "document_hash": "d939ed6653e40bf096f9ba9f67ecd3f5e345bc6f3c37a2d6de104fb5b3324c19",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d939ed6653e4-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: What do you think about the approach -- because I have had some of these cases. And I see -- I think words, words, words."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2006-06-484",
      "confidence": 0.94,
      "document_hash": "d939ed6653e40bf096f9ba9f67ecd3f5e345bc6f3c37a2d6de104fb5b3324c19",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d939ed6653e4-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: You indicated that the -- you know, the plaintiff had to do all this investigation. The whole point of this was that the defendants were being disadvantaged."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2006-06-484",
      "confidence": 0.93,
      "document_hash": "d939ed6653e40bf096f9ba9f67ecd3f5e345bc6f3c37a2d6de104fb5b3324c19",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d939ed6653e4-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: Mr. Miller, going back just to the word strong, forgetting the particularization from it, do you think you can categorize the strength in percentage terms? They have to be more than 50 percent? More than probable cause?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2006-06-484",
      "confidence": 0.96,
      "document_hash": "d939ed6653e40bf096f9ba9f67ecd3f5e345bc6f3c37a2d6de104fb5b3324c19",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d939ed6653e4-0002-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: You don't think they mean anything?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Carter G. Phillips (Petitioner)",
      "case_id": "2006-06-484",
      "confidence": 0.88,
      "document_hash": "d939ed6653e40bf096f9ba9f67ecd3f5e345bc6f3c37a2d6de104fb5b3324c19",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d939ed6653e4-0002-0010",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 52,
        "start": 52
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. PHILLIPS: Absolutely. Absolutely, Justice Ginsburg. There's no question that that's -- that that's what Congress had to have meant under these circumstances."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Carter G. Phillips (Petitioner)",
      "case_id": "2006-06-484",
      "confidence": 0.87,
      "document_hash": "d939ed6653e40bf096f9ba9f67ecd3f5e345bc6f3c37a2d6de104fb5b3324c19",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d939ed6653e4-0002-0011",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. PHILLIPS: I could be flip to say it's the right way. But I think the -- I mean the answer to the, the answer to why to choose our approach is because it is consistent with Matsushita and Monsanto and it will allow, Justice Breyer, to apply it in an individualized way, in a fashion that will give guidance to the lower courts."
    },
